.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt RMXOS-OWNER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rmxos-owner
.Nd establish the first owner of an rmxOS installation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -hostname Ar name
.Fl -user Ar name
.Fl -key Ar file
.Op Fl -altroot Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs as root, once, on an installation made without an owner.
It uses the same writer as
.Xr rmxos-install 8 ,
and its options have the installer's names and meanings:
an installation made with the installer's three owner options and one made
without them followed by
.Nm
with the same values hold the same owner files.
The default root is the running system;
.Fl -altroot
names an installed root with its Data datasets mounted, as the
installer's option of that name does.
All three values are required and use the installer's hostname,
lowercase user name (excluding root and toor), and single OpenSSH
public-key-line validators.
They are checked before any write.
It does not ask for or set a user password.
Before an owner is added, the template's empty root password permits
console access only; sshd refuses root and empty-password logins.
.Pp
An owner exists when
.Pa /private/var/db/rmxos/owner
exists, when
.Pa /private/etc/sysctl.conf
has a security.mac.do.rules setting, or when root's password begins with
*LOCKED*, including installations made by the earlier installer.
Such roots are refused without changing any files.
An existing account with the requested name is also refused.
The existing etc directory is locked to serialize owner writers without
creating a lock file during a refusal.
This command does not replace or remove owners.
.Pp
The steps run in this order, with paths below the root:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Xr pw 8
adds the user with no password, home
.Pa /Users/ Ns Ar name
and shell
.Pa /bin/sh .
.It
The home (mode 0755),
.Pa .ssh
(0700) and
.Pa .ssh/authorized_keys
(0600) holding the key are created, owned by that user's uid and gid.
.It
The user's
.Xr mdo 1
rule is added to
.Pa /private/etc/sysctl.conf .
.It
.Pa /private/etc/rc.conf
gets the hostname, DHCP, sshd and
.Xr mac_do 4
settings.
.It
On the running system only, that is with the root
.Pa / ,
the default and what
.Xr rmxos-owner-seed 8
passes at boot:
.Xr hostname 1
sets the host name,
.Xr kldload 8
loads mac_do unless it is loaded, and
.Xr sysctl 8
sets security.mac.do.rules to the rule of step 3, so the user can use
.Xr mdo 1
without a reboot.
With any other
.Fl -altroot ,
including another spelling of
.Pa / ,
nothing is applied: the files take effect at that system's next boot.
.It
Root's password is locked and the password databases are rebuilt with
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8 .
.It
The user name is written to
.Pa /private/var/db/rmxos/owner .
.El
.Pp
No file is left half written: each is written aside and renamed into place.
.Pp
.Xr pw 8
records the new account in the log its
.Xr pw.conf 5
names, a path on the system it runs on.
On the running system that is its own
.Pa /var/log/userlog .
For any other root the account is logged nowhere: step 1 gives
.Xr pw 8
a temporary
.Pa /private/var/run/rmxos-owner.pw.conf
below the root, holding the root's own
.Pa pw.conf
settings and
.Dq logfile = no ,
and removes it after the step.
.Sh RECOVERY
A failure prints one line naming its error.
A refusal leaves the owner files unchanged.
A failure in steps 1 to 5 leaves root as it was, so root still logs in
on the console.
It can leave the account and its group, the home and, after step 3, the
mdo rule; after a failure in step 5 the host name, mac_do and the live
rule may also be set, until the next boot or a retry, which sets them again.
A retry refuses while the account or the rule exists.
To clear them before a retry, as root on the console:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pw -V /private/etc userdel -n name
rm -r /Users/name
.Ed
.Pp
and delete the line it added to
.Pa /private/etc/sysctl.conf ,
which names the user's uid (here 1001):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
security.mac.do.rules="uid=1001>uid=0,gid=*,+gid=*"
.Ed
.Pp
.Pa rc.conf
needs no change: a retry rewrites those settings.
.Pp
A failure in step 6 or 7 leaves steps 1 to 5 done.
If root's line in
.Pa /private/etc/master.passwd
begins with *LOCKED*, finish by hand instead of retrying:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pwd_mkdb -d /private/etc -p /private/etc/master.passwd
echo name > /private/var/db/rmxos/owner
.Ed
.Pp
Otherwise root was not locked and is as it was: clear as above and retry.
For an installed root, prefix each of these paths with the
.Fl -altroot
directory;
.Xr pw 8
then logs on the system it runs on unless given
.Fl C
and a file holding
.Dq logfile = no .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the owner is added, 1 after a usage error, refusal or failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hostname 1 ,
.Xr mdo 1 ,
.Xr mac_do 4 ,
.Xr pw.conf 5 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8 ,
.Xr rmxos-install 8 ,
.Xr rmxos-owner-seed 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
